{"term":"biseksualistka","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"identities_orientations","subcategory":"bisexual","concept":"bisexual","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"clinical","crude":5,"technical":65,"recognition_pct_current":72,"recognition_pct_peak":72,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A woman who is sexually and/or romantically attracted to more than one gender; grammatically feminine noun form of 'biseksualista'.","english_term":"bisexual woman","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Grammatically feminine noun; masculine equivalent is 'biseksualista'. The adjectival form 'biseksualny/biseksualna' is also widely used."}
{"term":"transeksualny","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"identities_orientations","subcategory":"transgender_clinical","concept":"transgender","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"clinical","crude":5,"technical":72,"recognition_pct_current":68,"recognition_pct_peak":75,"peak_year":2010,"regional":null,"definition":"Adjective meaning transsexual; describing a person whose gender identity differs from their sex assigned at birth; masculine/neuter adjectival form of the older clinical term.","english_term":"transsexual","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Older clinical term, increasingly replaced by 'transpłciowy' as the preferred adjective in activist and institutional contexts. Maps to the older medical concept of transsexualism rather than the broader transgender umbrella."}
{"term":"transseksualista","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"identities_orientations","subcategory":"transgender_clinical","concept":"transgender","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"clinical","crude":5,"technical":65,"recognition_pct_current":62,"recognition_pct_peak":72,"peak_year":2008,"regional":null,"definition":"A transsexual person (grammatically masculine noun); older clinical term for a person whose gender identity differs from their sex assigned at birth.","english_term":"transsexual","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Grammatically masculine Polish noun; increasingly supplanted by 'osoba transpłciowa'. Older Polish medical literature sometimes used 'transseksualista' specifically for MTF individuals, a usage now considered imprecise and problematic."}
{"term":"transseksualistka","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"identities_orientations","subcategory":"transgender_clinical","concept":"transgender","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"clinical","crude":5,"technical":65,"recognition_pct_current":60,"recognition_pct_peak":68,"peak_year":2008,"regional":null,"definition":"A transsexual person (grammatically feminine noun); older clinical term for a person whose gender identity differs from their sex assigned at birth.","english_term":"transsexual","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Grammatically feminine Polish noun; increasingly supplanted by 'osoba transpłciowa'. Polish noun gender historically tracked birth sex rather than gender identity, creating terminological friction this term embodies."}
{"term":"transgender","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"identities_orientations","subcategory":"transgender_loanword","concept":"transgender","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"loanword","crude":5,"technical":50,"recognition_pct_current":80,"recognition_pct_peak":80,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Loanword from English; used as an uninflected adjective or noun in Polish to designate a person whose gender identity differs from their sex assigned at birth.","english_term":"transgender","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Used indeclinable in Polish ('osoba transgender'). The native equivalent 'transpłciowy/transpłciowa' is preferred in activist circles but 'transgender' achieves broader mainstream media recognition."}
{"term":"queer","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"identities_orientations","subcategory":"queer_umbrella","concept":"queer_identity","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"loanword","crude":10,"technical":20,"recognition_pct_current":62,"recognition_pct_peak":62,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Loanword from English used as an umbrella term for non-heterosexual and non-cisgender identities; used primarily in activist, academic, and progressive media contexts in Poland.","english_term":"queer","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Uninflected loanword. Recognition is uneven — younger urban Poles familiar with LGBTQ+ discourse know it well; older or rural speakers may not. The reclaimed activist sense is the dominant sense in Polish usage; the original English slur valence is largely absent."}
{"term":"panseksualny","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"identities_orientations","subcategory":"pansexual","concept":"pansexual","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"loanword","crude":5,"technical":58,"recognition_pct_current":52,"recognition_pct_peak":52,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Adjective meaning pansexual; describing sexual or romantic attraction to people regardless of sex or gender identity; masculine/neuter adjectival form.","english_term":"pansexual","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Calque from Greek 'pan-' plus 'seksualny'. Recognition limited primarily to LGBTQ+ community members and younger educated speakers. Feminine form is 'panseksualna'."}
{"term":"interseksualny","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"identities_orientations","subcategory":"intersex","concept":"intersex","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"clinical","crude":3,"technical":75,"recognition_pct_current":48,"recognition_pct_peak":48,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Adjective meaning intersex; describing a person born with sex characteristics that do not fit typical binary definitions of male or female; masculine/neuter adjectival form.","english_term":"intersex","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Clinical loanword. Low general recognition — intersex as a concept is considerably less visible in Polish public discourse than LGB or trans topics. Feminine form is 'interseksualna'."}
{"term":"niebinarny","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"identities_orientations","subcategory":"non_binary","concept":"non_binary","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"loanword","crude":4,"technical":52,"recognition_pct_current":58,"recognition_pct_peak":58,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Adjective meaning non-binary; describing a gender identity that exists outside or across the traditional binary of man and woman; masculine/neuter adjectival form.","english_term":"non-binary","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Calque from English 'non-binary' ('nie-' = non-, 'binarny' = binary). Polish's grammatically gendered system creates structural difficulties for non-binary speakers, an ongoing tension in Polish LGBTQ+ linguistic activism."}
{"term":"niebinarna","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"identities_orientations","subcategory":"non_binary","concept":"non_binary","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"loanword","crude":4,"technical":52,"recognition_pct_current":58,"recognition_pct_peak":58,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Feminine adjectival form meaning non-binary; describing a gender identity outside the traditional binary; applied when the referent is grammatically or socially coded as feminine.","english_term":"non-binary","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Feminine form of 'niebinarny'; the existence of gendered adjectival forms itself creates paradox when describing non-binary persons — a structural irony debated in Polish LGBTQ+ linguistic circles."}
{"term":"osoba transpłciowa","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"identities_orientations","subcategory":"transgender_respectful","concept":"transgender","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"clinical","crude":2,"technical":72,"recognition_pct_current":68,"recognition_pct_peak":68,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'transgender person'; the most respectful and widely preferred modern Polish term for a transgender individual, using the native prefix 'trans-' combined with 'płciowy' (of sex/gender).","english_term":"transgender person","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Two-word phrase (noun 'osoba' = person + adjective 'transpłciowa'). The native adjective 'transpłciowy/transpłciowa' is preferred in activist and institutional contexts over the loanword 'transgender'. The noun 'osoba' (person) makes the phrase grammatically inclusive without foregrounding birth sex."}
{"term":"pedał","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"identities_orientations","subcategory":"gay_slur","concept":"gay_man_slur","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":92,"technical":0,"recognition_pct_current":99,"recognition_pct_peak":99,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Extremely offensive Polish slur for a gay man; the single most prevalent homophobic insult in Polish; severity roughly equivalent to American English 'faggot'.","english_term":"faggot","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Literal meaning is 'pedal' (bicycle or piano pedal); slur origin disputed — likely a phonetic shortening of 'pederasta'. Ubiquitous across all age groups and regions; the default homophobic insult in Poland. Deeply embedded in everyday vernacular."}
{"term":"pedzio","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"identities_orientations","subcategory":"gay_slur","concept":"gay_man_slur","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":85,"technical":0,"recognition_pct_current":88,"recognition_pct_peak":88,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Informal diminutive variant of 'pedał'; a highly offensive slur for a gay man; slightly more colloquial in feel than 'pedał' but equally offensive in meaning.","english_term":"faggot","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Polish diminutive suffix '-io' morphologically softens the register but the slur remains highly offensive. Used interchangeably with 'pedał'; the diminutive may add a contemptuous or dismissive edge in some contexts."}
{"term":"ciota","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"identities_orientations","subcategory":"gay_slur_effeminate","concept":"effeminate_gay_man","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":78,"technical":0,"recognition_pct_current":85,"recognition_pct_peak":85,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A derogatory term for an effeminate gay man; also used as a general insult for a weak, cowardly, or contemptible man; foregrounds unmanliness and effeminacy alongside homosexuality.","english_term":"fairy / pansy","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Emphasizes effeminacy and weakness more than homosexuality per se; can insult straight men by impugning masculinity. In some Polish dialects, 'ciota' also means 'aunt' or 'old woman', adding layered meaning. Slightly less severe than 'pedał' but widely used."}
{"term":"cwel","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"identities_orientations","subcategory":"gay_slur_prison","concept":"gay_man_slur","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":95,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":88,"recognition_pct_peak":88,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Prison slang for a man who has been sexually subjugated or used for sex by other inmates; outside prison, an extremely offensive slur for a gay man or any weak, submissive, or contemptible person.","english_term":"punk (prison slang)","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Originates in Polish prison and criminal subculture (gwara więzienna); carries connotations of sexual dehumanization and forced submission beyond mere homosexuality. Transferred to mainstream speech as a general homophobic insult. Among the most stigmatizing and dehumanizing terms in the Polish vernacular."}
{"term":"homek","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"identities_orientations","subcategory":"gay_slur_informal","concept":"homosexual_slur","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":62,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":60,"recognition_pct_peak":72,"peak_year":2003,"regional":null,"definition":"Informal derogatory term for a homosexual or gay person; derived colloquially from 'homoseksualista'; less severe than 'pedał' but still offensive.","english_term":"homo","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Diminutive/clipped form derived from 'homo(seksualista)'. Somewhat dated, having peaked in the 1990s–early 2000s; still understood and used but less frequent than 'pedał'. Primarily refers to gay men."}
{"term":"lesba","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"identities_orientations","subcategory":"lesbian_slur","concept":"lesbian_slur","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":65,"technical":0,"recognition_pct_current":88,"recognition_pct_peak":88,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Derogatory or informal term for a lesbian woman; truncated from 'lesbijka'; perceived as offensive in most contexts but partially reclaimed by some younger lesbians in Poland.","english_term":"dyke","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Formed by truncating 'lesbijka' to 'lesb-' and applying the standard feminine '-a' ending, giving it a morphologically natural feel. Reclamation parallels the English reclamation of 'dyke'. Used both as an external slur and, increasingly, as a self-identifier in activist spaces."}
{"term":"lesbo","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"identities_orientations","subcategory":"lesbian_slur","concept":"lesbian_slur","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":65,"technical":0,"recognition_pct_current":82,"recognition_pct_peak":82,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Derogatory term for a lesbian woman; loanword from English 'lesbo' retained unchanged; used interchangeably with 'lesba' in Polish slang.","english_term":"lesbo","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"English loanword retained without modification. Slightly more anglicized in register than 'lesba'; the two forms coexist without meaningful distinction in meaning or severity. The direct borrowing makes it feel marginally more recent or media-influenced."}
{"term":"zboczeńiec","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"identities_orientations","subcategory":"pervert_slur","concept":"pervert","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":58,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":82,"recognition_pct_peak":82,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A pejorative term for a sexual pervert or deviant; describes any person whose sexual behavior or orientation is judged abnormal or morally offensive; standard orthographic spelling with diacritical ń.","english_term":"pervert","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Derived from 'zboczenie' (deviation, perversion). Frequently used to frame LGBTQ+ people as deviant. This is the standard orthographic form with the diacritic; 'zboczeniec' (below) is a common variant without it."}
{"term":"zboczeniec","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"identities_orientations","subcategory":"pervert_slur","concept":"pervert","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":58,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":82,"recognition_pct_peak":82,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A pejorative term for a sexual pervert or deviant; variant spelling of 'zboczeńiec' without the diacritical mark; identical in meaning and usage.","english_term":"pervert","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Non-diacritical variant of 'zboczeńiec'; used interchangeably, especially in informal digital communication where diacritics are often omitted. No difference in meaning, register, or connotation."}
{"term":"sodomita","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"identities_orientations","subcategory":"sodomite_religious","concept":"sodomite","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"moral_religious","crude":42,"technical":40,"recognition_pct_current":52,"recognition_pct_peak":52,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A religious and moral term for a man who practices sodomy; borrowed from Latin via Church tradition; used in Catholic theological contexts to condemn anal sex, especially between men.","english_term":"sodomite","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Poland's strong Catholic tradition gives this term more vitality than in secular Western countries; it appears regularly in conservative Catholic and right-wing political rhetoric. Relatively rare in everyday secular speech but carries high moral charge when deployed."}
{"term":"pederasta","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"identities_orientations","subcategory":"gay_slur_historical","concept":"gay_man_slur","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"dated_slang","crude":75,"technical":25,"recognition_pct_current":78,"recognition_pct_peak":88,"peak_year":1980,"regional":null,"definition":"A slur for a gay man; technically means pederast (adult male who has sex with boys) but used in everyday Polish primarily as a general derogatory term for any gay man, with strong moral condemnation.","english_term":"pederast / faggot (in slur usage)","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"The semantic conflation of homosexuality with pederasty reflects historical pathologization of same-sex attraction. Somewhat dated compared to 'pedał', which is more current in everyday speech; still understood and used by older speakers and in conservative or clerical contexts."}
{"term":"przegięty","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"identities_orientations","subcategory":"effeminate_slur","concept":"effeminate_man_slur","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":70,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":78,"recognition_pct_peak":78,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'overly bent'; derogatory adjective for a stereotypically effeminate or flamboyantly gay man; refers to exaggerated feminine mannerisms; masculine adjectival form.","english_term":"limp-wristed / queeny","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"From 'przegiąć się' (to bend too far, to overdo it). The non-sexual meaning 'overdone' or 'too much' coexists in Polish slang; the homophobic meaning is dominant when applied to a person. Applied specifically to camp affectation rather than homosexuality per se."}
{"term":"przegięta","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"identities_orientations","subcategory":"effeminate_slur","concept":"effeminate_man_slur","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":65,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":72,"recognition_pct_peak":72,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Feminine adjectival form of 'przegięty'; applied to a woman perceived as excessively camp, dramatically queer-presenting, or stereotypically lesbian in mannerism.","english_term":"queeny / over-the-top","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Feminine form of 'przegięty'. Slur force somewhat lower than the masculine form, which has a more established homophobic tradition. When applied to women, connotes stereotypical lesbian presentation or general excessive camp behavior. Can also be used non-sexually to mean 'overdramatic'."}
{"term":"babochłop","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"identities_orientations","subcategory":"masculine_woman_slur","concept":"butch_lesbian_slur","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":72,"technical":0,"recognition_pct_current":68,"recognition_pct_peak":78,"peak_year":1995,"regional":null,"definition":"Compound noun meaning 'woman-man'; a derogatory term for a masculine-presenting woman, butch lesbian, or gender-nonconforming female person; implies failure to meet feminine norms.","english_term":"he-she / mannish woman","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Compound of 'baba' (woman, often pejorative/rustic) + 'chłop' (man, peasant). Applied to women with masculine appearance, voice, or mannerisms; frequently directed at butch lesbians and transgender men. The 'baba' component adds a class-coded contempt absent in English 'butch'. Somewhat dated; peak usage circa mid-1990s."}
